## 14. v24e: two follow-up findings from Devin Review on PR #589 (text only in paper/stam-m_ja.tex)

Scope: no implementation, evaluation value, SSOT value, figure PNG, table value or saved LLM output changed; no LLM
call re-run. Both findings were checked against llm/sql_generator.py (_score_sql_candidate, score constants),
llm/domain_validator.py (validate_result_rows, penalty cap 30), llm/reranker.py (weight 0.4), safety/sql_guard.py,
api/main.py, scripts/eval_ablation.py (exec_fn, normalize_limit) and evaluation/metrics.py (normalize_limit).

1. n-best mechanical score (Section 2.4 omitted items that affect candidate ordering). The manuscript now lists every
   component with its points: SQLGuard pass 30 (a failing candidate scores 0 and is not trial-executed), trial
   execution success 20, non-empty result 10, condition coverage up to 20 (fraction of extracted prototype / element /
   stability / sort conditions whose clause appears in the SQL; 20 when no such condition was extracted), row-count
   plausibility 10 for 1-500 rows / 5 above 500, DISTINCT 5, LIMIT 5; after a successful trial execution the
   materials-domain validation of the result rows (5 rules: prototype vs formula type, lattice-constant bounds,
   E_hull vs stability condition, gamma-prime base elements, site-label validity) subtracts up to 30 points (floor 0).
   Stated to be a candidate-ranking design value, not an evaluation metric.
2. SQLGuard three-way classification vs evaluation-time execution (Section 2.7 and supplement S1 list preamble).
   The reject / warn / rewrite execution control (rejected SQL not executed, rewritten SQL executed) is the behaviour of
   the shipped execution layer (execute_sql() used by the API and UI). In the evaluation SQLGuard acts only as a
   component of the n-best candidate score: failing candidates score 0 and are not trial-executed, but
   _score_sql_candidate() passes the original candidate SQL (not validation["sql"]) to execute_fn, and
   eval_ablation.py executes the selected SQL after normalize_limit() (adds LIMIT 10000 only when no LIMIT is
   present; does not cap a larger explicit LIMIT) regardless of the SQLGuard verdict, including the case in which every
   candidate failed. The manuscript now says so and describes SQLGuard's contribution to the evaluation values as
   indirect (through candidate selection), with no_guard defined as "validation treated as always passing in the
   candidate score". The supplement list preamble no longer implies that every path executes the rewritten SQL.

